From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Salty <99386653+PjSalty@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 19:27:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] migrations: strip the dead Data keys without SQLite
 JSON functions

Intent: 12.1's StripEmbeddedLinkedChildren (a CoreInitialisation code
migration every existing database runs) removed LinkedChildren,
ExtraIds and SupportsExternalTransfer from BaseItems.Data with
json_remove()/json_valid() through ExecuteSqlRaw. Those are SQLite
functions: on PostgreSQL the statement fails with 42883 ("function
json_valid(text) does not exist"), the migration service restores the
pre-migration dump and the migration Job exits non-zero. Do the edit in
provider-neutral C# instead: collect the ids whose Data mentions one of
the keys, then per batch of 500 load Id + Data, drop the three
top-level keys with System.Text.Json.Nodes and write changed rows back
with ExecuteUpdate inside one transaction per batch. Every read is
materialised before the writes that follow it (Npgsql allows one active
command per connection). Blobs are re-serialized with JsonDefaults, the
options they are written with, so kept content comes out byte for byte.
A blob that is not valid JSON is left alone and counted in a warning
(json_valid() skipped it too); one that is not a JSON object has no
top-level keys to drop and is left alone. Includes a test that runs the
old SQLite statement on a twin of every row and requires identical
results on 1,007 rows across two batch boundaries (escapes, nested
keys, key names inside values, malformed JSON, arrays, nulls), under an
interceptor that refuses a write while a reader is open.
Ceiling: one UPDATE per changed row (shortcut: marker in the code) and
one round trip per batch of reads instead of a single statement; fine
for a one-time migration.
Unlike the old LIKE filter, matching is case-sensitive, which is exactly
the set json_remove() could change.
Drop when: upstream rewrites the routine without provider-specific SQL.

Patch: 0021
